# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/005_bool_conversions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 005_bool_conversions
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = _Bool conversion from integer, unsigned, long long and floating scalars and back, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every conversion is well defined. Conversion of any scalar to _Bool is defined to yield 0
if the value compares equal to 0 and 1 otherwise, so no value is ever out of range and the
floating source 0.25 is compared against zero rather than converted to an integer. Reading
a _Bool back yields only 0 or 1, so no arithmetic here can overflow a signed type. No shift
appears. No aliasing violation, no uninitialized read, no object modified twice between
sequence points, at most one side-effecting argument per call, no dependence on padding.
Every conversion is written as an explicit cast, which keeps the strict warning gate clean
without any deviation while still exercising the conversion. The _Generic controlling
expressions are unevaluated, so they report promoted types without converting at run time.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Plain char is never used and no object is declared plain char, because plain char
signedness was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64.
long and unsigned long are never used, because their width is 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes
on the other three targets. No pointer value is printed.
sizeof(_Bool) is deliberately never printed: its value is not fixed by the standard, and
printing it would risk reporting a purely implementation-defined difference as a finding.
Only conversion semantics are asserted, and those are identical on all four targets.
No header is included, so the _Bool keyword is used directly rather than stdbool.h, which
also keeps the program reproducible from this record alone.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_from_zero=0
fold_from_one=1
fold_from_neg=1
fold_from_256=1
fold_from_u_high=1
fold_from_l_high=1
fold_from_double=1
fold_from_double_zero=0
fold_back_to_int=11
fold_sum=2
fold_not=1
fold_negate=-1
run_from_zero=0
run_from_one=1
run_from_neg=1
run_from_256=1
run_from_u_high=1
run_from_l_high=1
run_from_double=1
run_from_double_zero=0
run_back_to_int=11
run_sum=2
run_not=1
run_negate=-1
bool_promotes=int
bool_sum_type=int
END
